{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794484540,"nonce":"GhlMcFGSkBY6eAgzoT3my5ruGaM=","message":"Text quality, clips and the remaining fallbacks, local in 1e213504772347ae99309a4c40a804f51e967568 (not pushed).\n\nText (measured with a gradient-energy sharpness metric in the harness, against text laid out natively or painted statically)\n- Text under a motion container or a CSS transform that only moves and scales it was placed at fractional device positions at any scale, so it softened, most at 1x: a label under a motion container was 0.75 as sharp as the same static label at 1x (0.86 at 2x). Quads are now folded to their bitmap size and rounded to whole pixels while the node is at rest: 1.00 at both densities.\n- Scaled-up text was a small bitmap magnified. It is now laid out at the base size and rasterized at the size it is shown, from the spring's target in table mode, refreshed in place; a zoomed label is as sharp as native text.\n- A label settled after an animation at a fractional offset was 0.81 as sharp as a full paint of that pose (1x). Glyphs are now baked at the pose the node settles at when it stops and return to the painted pose and slot when it moves: 1.00.\n- A node at rest scaled slightly down (a pressed button) is rasterized at that exact scale. The pressed-state snapshots of cn button and toggle changed by their label's edges; I blessed those 8 machine-local goldens.\n- Not addressed: static text's own rendering on a 1x display compared with other apps. Static glyphs were already snapped; the shader already applies a coverage gamma of 0.7; the rasterizer (swash) hints CFF outlines only. If static text is still soft to you, the next things to try are stem darkening and hinting TrueType outlines, and that needs a side-by-side judgement by eye.\n\nClips\n- The painter records the clip in force when a binding begins. A primitive's clip equal to it belongs to an ancestor and stays; one that differs is moved by the rows from the owning node up (a clip lane in node_slot, with a CPU twin). Rectangles only, and not under a turn. A card in a clipped box and a card that clips its own children, scaling as it moves, run on rows.\n\nFallbacks that remain (each still repaints the whole frame, as before)\n- An opacity group with text in it (cross-stream captured group); SVG in a bound subtree; a layer with effects, blend or a 3D transform; a stretch with a turn, or a turn over a clip that has to move; a clip that moves and is not a rectangle; chains over four; CSS animations (not on the table at all). The compositor's patch result is all or nothing, so any one of these in a frame costs every tile its rows. Removing them is the P2 work that is left, and the captured-group case is the largest.\n\nProcess note: a script of mine truncated node_rows.rs to empty mid-session; restored from the last commit and the uncommitted edits replayed. No other effect.","files":null}]}